    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on a hypertrophy program for a client and need to explain the biochemistry a bit better. can you give me a breakdown of how leucine specifically triggers the mTOR pathway and how a caloric surplus relates to maintaining a positive nitrogen balance for muscle protein synthesis?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Leucine, a key amino acid, acts as a metabolic switch, signaling to the mTOR pathway, which is the central regulator for muscle protein synthesis. It does this by binding to specific receptors, effectively telling the cell, \"Hey, there's enough fuel to build muscle!\" A caloric surplus provides the necessary energy substrate—the \"fuel\" for the construction crew—allowing the body to allocate excess energy towards building new muscle tissue, which is fundamentally what maintains a positive nitrogen balance. Without enough energy, the body has to prioritize survival, which can lead to muscle breakdown, even if protein intake is adequate.",
      "source": "target",
