    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. could you go into a bit more detail on the actual mechanism? like, how does the leucine signal actually translate at the cellular level to activate those proteins, and what happens to the nitrogen balance specifically if the surplus is too low?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The cellular mechanism involves leucine acting as an activator, often by inhibiting the TSC1/TSC2 complex, which normally acts as a brake on mTORC1. When leucine levels are high enough, this brake is lifted, allowing mTORC1 to become active and phosphorylate downstream targets like S6K1 and 4E-BP1. If the caloric surplus is too low, the body enters a state of energy deficit, which can trigger catabolic pathways—the breakdown of tissue—to meet energy demands. This shifts the nitrogen balance toward negative, meaning the body is losing more nitrogen (and thus muscle mass) than it is retaining from protein intake.",
      "source": "target",
