    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can you explain the role of insulin in this whole process? i'm trying to understand how insulin sensitivity might influence the leucine/mTOR interaction and if that changes the required surplus for keeping that nitrogen balance positive.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Insulin plays a crucial role, acting as a major anabolic signal, much like a well-fed, energetic state, which is what a surplus provides. Insulin sensitivity dictates how effectively your cells respond to this signal, and when it's poor, the whole anabolic machinery stalls, making the mTOR pathway less responsive to leucine. Poor insulin sensitivity means that even with sufficient leucine and energy, the signal transmission is weak, which can effectively mimic a state of energy scarcity, requiring a larger caloric surplus to overcome the cellular resistance. This resistance can make maintaining a positive nitrogen balance much harder, requiring more diligent fueling to keep the muscle-building processes running smoothly.",
      "source": "target",
